target:
def Target : Prop :=
  ∀ (X Y : Type) (N : S1 X Y) (T : ℕ),
    (N.d14 T).D9 ∧ S1.D5 T (N.d14 T) N ∧ (N.d14 T).d13 = T * N.d13

b_raw:
{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Finite time horizon T: P2 as worded in P-D53 (and the general statement in P-D52a) asserts the existence of a feedforward system with 'the same input-output function' without a horizon restriction, whereas Target introduces a finite horizon T, makes the unrolled system N.d14 T depend on T, and asserts agreement only on input sequences of length at most T (S1.D5 T rather than unbounded equivalence S1.D6). Although this is strictly weaker than unbounded equivalence (and declared by the author), it aligns with the finite-duration unrolling-through-time justification in P-H10 (e.g., 100 copies for 1 second at 10 ms resolution) and the behavioural-experiment framing in P-D52a."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Idealized system model: S1 X Y models systems as finite-node, discrete-time, deterministic, synchronous state machines with a fixed initial state, whereas the source passages speak more generally of recurrent and feedforward networks or physical systems (which could be continuous-time, asynchronous, or stochastic, though P-H10 already idealizes to discrete timesteps)."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Quantification over all systems: Target quantifies over all systems N : S1 X Y (including non-recurrent ones and edge cases such as T = 0 or empty X), whereas the source passages restrict attention to recurrent networks/systems. This is a harmless strengthening (and declared by the author)."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Explicit witness instead of existential claim: P-D53 (P2) states existentially that 'there exist feedforward systems' with the same input-output function, whereas Target asserts the properties for the single explicitly defined system N.d14 T. This is a harmless strengthening that reflects P-H10's specific unrolling construction."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Functional vs. structural feedforwardness and operational details of d14: Feedforwardness (D9) is formalized as acyclicity of single-node functional sensitivity (D7/D8) rather than physical wiring, and to handle variable sequence lengths up to T with a fixed readout at layer T - 1, N.d14 T broadcasts each input simultaneously to all T layers (so shorter sequences of length L < T are carried through layers T - L to T - 1 rather than starting at layer 0, and T = 0 yields an empty network with a constant readout)."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Node-count conjunct vs. copy structure: The third conjunct of Target explicitly asserts only the cardinality equality (N.d14 T).d13 = T * N.d13, leaving the fact that N.d14 T actually consists of T copies of N's units (with matching local state spaces and transition rules) inside the definition of d14 itself."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Target faithfully formalizes the recurrent-to-feedforward direction of P2 via unrolling through time as described in the sources, establishing acyclicity, T-step input-output equivalence, and T-fold unit scaling for an explicit unrolled construction. The main caveats are the explicit finite horizon T (implicit in P-H10's unrolling justification but absent from P2's bare wording) and standard modeling idealizations to finite, discrete-time, deterministic, synchronous systems."
